-- title:
--   Pushout of two surjections of commutative bialgebras
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field and let $A$, $B$, $C$ be commutative rings each carrying the structure of a bialgebra over $k$. Let $\pi : A \to B$ and $\mu : A \to C$ be bialgebra homomorphisms over $k$ (so $k$-algebra maps compatible with comultiplication and counit), and suppose both $\pi$ and $\mu$ are surjective as functions. The conclusion asserts the existence of a type $D$ in the same universe as $B$, equipped with a commutative ring structure and a $k$-bialgebra structure, together with a bialgebra homomorphism $\rho : B \to D$ over $k$, such that $\rho$ is surjective and the kernel of $\rho$, taken as the kernel of the underlying $k$-algebra map, equals the ideal of $B$ generated by the image under $\pi$ of the kernel of the underlying $k$-algebra map of $\mu$, i.e. $\ker \rho = \pi(\ker \mu) \cdot B$ in the sense of `Ideal.map`. No finiteness, flatness or Hopf-algebra (antipode) hypothesis is imposed, and $D$ is produced only up to the stated properties: no universal property of $D$ is asserted.
--
--   Dually, for $G = \operatorname{Spec} A$ with closed subschemes $H$ and $N$ cut out by the bi-ideals $\ker\pi$ and $\ker\mu$, the bialgebra $D$ is the coordinate ring $B \otimes_A C$ of the intersection $H \cap N$, presented as a quotient of $B$ by the ideal generated by the image of $\ker\mu$. It is used in the analysis of Witt-vector homomorphism schemes, where a surjection with exactly this kernel is what the argument consumes.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Bialgebra_exists_surjective_bialgHom_ker_eq_map_ker.lean

